Amazon SNS — Data Limitations

Scope: Constraints on message data (payload, encoding, structure, retention) for application-to-application use of Amazon SNS. Does not cover throughput quotas, resource quotas, or A2P (SMS/email/push) channels.

Last verified: September 2026, against the AWS General Reference and the Amazon SNS Developer Guide.

1. Payload size
Limit	Value	Adjustable
Maximum message size	262,144 bytes (256 KiB)	No
Maximum header size	16,384 bytes (16 KiB)	No
Extended payload maximum	2 GB	N/A
Billing unit	64 KB per request	N/A

Notes

The 256 KiB limit is measured in bytes, not characters. Multi-byte UTF-8 characters (CJK, emoji, accented Latin) consume 2–4 bytes each, so payloads that appear within limits by character count may still be rejected.
The 2 GB maximum is not native to SNS. It is provided by the Amazon SNS Extended Client Libraries, which store the payload in Amazon S3 and publish a pointer. All subscribers must use the corresponding client library to resolve the pointer.
Billing is per 64 KB chunk. A single 256 KB publish is billed as four requests, and this applies to both publishes and deliveries.
2. Encoding and content type
Messages must be UTF-8 encoded strings.
Permitted content: XML, JSON, or unformatted text.
Binary data is not supported in the message body. Binary content must be base64-encoded before publishing, which increases size by approximately 33% against the 256 KiB limit.
Binary is supported natively only as a message attribute with DataType: Binary.
3. Message attributes

Message attributes are optional structured metadata sent alongside — but separate from — the message body.

Constraint	Value
Counts against message size limit	Yes — name, type, and value all included in the 256 KiB total
Supported data types	String, String.Array, Number, Binary
Maximum attributes (SQS + Raw Message Delivery)	10
Maximum attributes (all other cases)	No fixed count; bounded by the 256 KiB size limit

Notes

Attribute name, type, and value must each be non-empty and non-null.
Attributes are transmitted only when MessageStructure is String, not JSON.
For Amazon SQS subscriptions with Raw Message Delivery enabled, messages carrying more than 10 attributes are discarded as client-side errors. The publisher receives no failure indication. To send more than 10 attributes, disable Raw Message Delivery.
When an attribute is used for subscription filtering, its value must be a valid JSON string.
4. Structural field limits
Field	Constraint
Topic name	1–256 characters. ASCII letters, numbers, underscores, and hyphens only. FIFO topic names must end with the .fifo suffix.
Subject	UTF-8 text, fewer than 100 characters, no line breaks or control characters.
MessageGroupId (FIFO only)	Maximum 128 characters. Alphanumeric and punctuation. Required on every message; publish fails if omitted.
MessageDeduplicationId (FIFO only)	Maximum 128 characters. Same character set. Required unless ContentBasedDeduplication is enabled.
Batch entry Id	Maximum 80 characters. Must be unique within the batch.
Messages per PublishBatch request	10 entries.

JSON message structure

When MessageStructure is set to json, the JSON object must contain a default key. Keys corresponding to supported transport protocols must map to simple JSON string values; nested objects are not permitted at the top level.

5. Subscription filter policy limits
Constraint	Value
Maximum policy size	256 KB
Maximum number of keys	5
Maximum combination of values	150
Numeric matching range	-1,000,000,000 to 1,000,000,000
Numeric precision	5 digits after the decimal point
Wildcard complexity ceiling	100 points
Filter policies per topic	200 (adjustable)
Filter policies per account	10,000 (adjustable)

Notes

Total combination of values is calculated by multiplying the number of values in each array in the policy.
String matching is case-sensitive.
Numeric values outside the supported range fail to match silently rather than raising an error.
The policy must be a valid UTF-8 JSON string containing only quoted strings, numbers, and the unquoted keywords true, false, and null.
The per-topic policy limit does not block subscription creation via Subscribe; the call fails only when the filter policy is attached (via Subscribe or SetSubscriptionAttributes).
6. Data retention

Standard topics do not retain data. There is no storage, no replay capability, and no consumer polling model. A message exists only for the duration of its delivery attempts and is discarded thereafter.

Endpoint type	Retry budget before discard
Amazon SQS, AWS Lambda	100,015 attempts over 23 days
HTTP/S	Customer-defined; total policy retry time capped at 3,600 seconds
SMTP, SMS, mobile push	50 attempts over 6 hours

If all delivery attempts fail and no dead-letter queue is attached to the subscription, the message is permanently lost. DLQs attach at the subscription level, not the topic level.

FIFO topics are the sole exception. Archive and replay policies retain messages for a configurable retention window and allow replay to new or existing subscriptions. No equivalent exists for standard topics.

7. Encryption and data protection
Server-side encryption is available using AWS KMS with either an AWS managed or customer managed key.
Data protection policies can scan and redact PII from message bodies in transit.
PutDataProtectionPolicy is throttled to 1 transaction per second in all commercial regions.
Content-based deduplication on FIFO topics generates a SHA-256 hash from the message body only, excluding message attributes. Two messages with identical bodies but different attributes are treated as duplicates, and only one is delivered.
8. Design implications
Constraint	Implication
No retention on standard topics	Attach a DLQ to every subscription that carries data you cannot afford to lose.
At-least-once delivery, no ordering	Consumers must be idempotent and tolerant of out-of-order arrival.
256 KiB ceiling	Use claim-check (S3 pointer) for larger payloads rather than relying on the Extended Client Library, unless all consumers can adopt it.
Silent discard at >10 attributes with raw delivery	Validate attribute count before publish; failures are not surfaced to the publisher.
64 KB billing chunks	Large payloads cost 4× per publish and per delivery. Payload size is a cost lever, not just a technical limit.
Body-only deduplication hash	Do not rely on attributes to differentiate otherwise-identical FIFO messages.

